Once the kinematics (\(p_{T}\), \(\eta\), and \(\phi\)) of the parent hadron obtained, the kinematics of the daughter electrons are determined by the decay kinematics. The electron pair mass depends on the parent particle and the decay mode (two-body decay or Dalitz decay). The electron pair mass of two-body decay follows a narrow Breit-Wigner distribution as given in Eq. 4.9

\[\frac{dN}{dM_{ee}}=\frac{2\Gamma_{0}}{(M_{ee}-M_{h})^{2}+\Gamma_{0}^{2}/4} \tag{4.9}\]

where the \(\Gamma_{0}\) represents the PDG [103] width, and \(M_{h}\) is the mass of the hadron which decays into the dielectron. The electron pair mass (\(M_{ee}\)) is constrained to [\(2m_{e}\), 4 \(GeV/c^{2}\)], where the \(m_{e}\) is the electron mass. The electron pair mass of Dalitz decay follows the Kroll-Wada formula [104] as given in Eq. 4.10,

\[\frac{dN}{dM_{ee}}=PS\cdot|F(M_{ee}^{2})|^{2}\cdot QED \tag{4.10}\]

Where PS is the phase space term defined in Eq. 4.11. The \(M_{h}\) is mass of the hadron which undergoes a Dalitz decay process (\(h\to Xe^{+}e^{-}\)) and \(X\) is the third daughter particle with a mass \(M_{X}\). if \(X\) is massless (e.g. \(\gamma\) in \(\pi^{0}\), \(\eta\), \(\eta^{\prime}\) Dalitz decay), the phase space term simplifies to Eq. 4.12.

\[PS=\left((1+\frac{M_{ee}^{2}}{M_{h}^{2}-M_{X}^{2}})^{2}-\frac{4M_{h}^{2}M_{ee} ^{2}}{(M_{h}^{2}-M_{X}^{2})^{2}}\right)^{\frac{3}{2}} \tag{4.11}\]

\[PS=\left(1-\frac{M_{ee}^{2}}{M_{h}^{2}}\right)^{3} \tag{4.12}\]

The QED term is described by Eq. 4.13, where N represents a degeneracy factor that depends on how many photons can convert. N is 4 for \(\omega\) and \(\phi\) while it is 2 for other hadrons undergoing Dalitz decay process, involved in this analysis. The \(\alpha\) is the fine-structure constant (\(\sim\)1/137).

\[QED=\frac{N\cdot\alpha}{3\pi}\sqrt{1-\frac{4m_{e}^{2}}{M_{ee}^{2}}}\left(1+ \frac{2m_{e}^{2}}{M_{ee}^{2}}\right)\frac{1}{M_{ee}} \tag{4.13}\]

